First, I remember that when selecting people for different positions where the order matters, it's a permutation problem. Because being president is different from being vice-president, etc. So each position is distinct.

Let me break it down step by step. For the first position, president, there are 20 possible members to choose from. Once the president is selected, they can't be vice-president or treasurer. So for the vice-president position, there are 19 remaining members. Then, after selecting the president and vice-president, the treasurer has to be someone else, so there are 18 members left.

So the total number of ways would be the product of these choices. That is, 20 multiplied by 19 multiplied by 18. Let me check that logic again. Yeah, because for each choice of president, there are 19 choices for vice-president, and for each of those combinations, 18 choices for treasurer. So the multiplication principle applies here.

Alternatively, I can think of this as permutations of 20 members taken 3 at a time. The formula for permutations is P(n, k) = n! / (n - k)!, where n is the total number of items, and k is the number of positions. Plugging in n=20 and k=3, we get P(20,3) = 20! / (20-3)! = 20! / 17!.

But 20! divided by 17! is the same as 20 × 19 × 18 × 17! / 17! which simplifies to 20 × 19 × 18. So that's the same as my initial calculation.

